---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Ladder from '../components/Ladder.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const topics = (await getCollection('topics')).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order
);

const PLACEMENTS = [
  { slug: 'meaning', rung: '5', why: 'A meaning you did not construct is not yours.' },
  { slug: 'purpose', rung: '3 · 5', why: 'Choose the stake yourself; pursue it with every tool available.' },
  { slug: 'curiosity', rung: '4', why: 'Guess first, then let the machine attack the guess.' },
  { slug: 'judgment', rung: '4 · 5', why: 'Anything you would have to defend, you reach unaided first.' },
  { slug: 'responsibility', rung: '2 ⚠', why: 'Rung two is where crumple zones form: liability without capacity.' },
  { slug: 'cognitive-fitness', rung: '5', why: 'Scheduled unaided reps. Training load, not productivity.' },
  { slug: 'education', rung: '4 · 5', why: 'Attempt, then adversarial critique. Reps through the hollowed middle.' },
  { slug: 'relationships', rung: '5', why: 'Automate the gesture and you have voided the gesture.' },
  { slug: 'work', rung: '2 · 3', why: 'Where the economic value is — and where your exposure is measured.' },
  { slug: 'civilization', rung: '3 · 4', why: 'Machine-assisted analysis; human choice of what to value.' },
];
const byId = Object.fromEntries(topics.map((t) => [t.id, t.data]));
---

<Base
  title="The Delegation Ladder — Human in the AI Era"
  description="A five-rung rubric for deciding what to hand to a machine and what to keep: cede, approve, collaborate, critique, reserve."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="lhead">
      <p class="label">The framework</p>
      <h1>The Delegation Ladder</h1>
      <p class="lhead__sub">
        Most advice about AI is binary — use it or don't. That framing is
        useless, because the same person should be on different rungs for
        different tasks in the same afternoon. This is the rubric the rest of
        the site runs on.
      </p>
    </header>

    <Ladder caption="Sienna and teal mark the axis: teal is machine-led, sienna is human-held. Every essay places its subject on these rungs." />

    <div class="lbody">
      <h2>How to choose a rung</h2>
      <p>
        Three questions, in order. They take under a minute and they resolve
        most cases.
      </p>

      <ol class="rules">
        <li>
          <span class="rules__q">Can I verify the output independently and cheaply?</span>
          <span class="rules__a">
            If yes — arithmetic, a fact with a source, code with a test — rungs one
            and two are fine. If verifying requires the very expertise the
            machine replaced, you are in the
            <a class="link" href="/topics/judgment">verification gap</a> and rungs
            one and two are not available to you, however convenient they feel.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="rules__q">Would I have to defend this?</span>
          <span class="rules__a">
            If your name goes on it, or a person is affected by it, rung four is
            the floor. Form your own position, record it, <em>then</em> consult.
            The sequencing is the whole safeguard — it neutralises commission
            error, which is the dominant failure mode.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="rules__q">Is this a skill I need to keep?</span>
          <span class="rules__a">
            Two tests: is it <strong>generative</strong> (does it build other
            capacities), and is it required to <strong>evaluate its own
            output</strong>? If both, budget rung-five reps on it deliberately.
            If neither, delegate it without guilt — most skills are in this
            category and pretending otherwise is just puritanism.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>The inversion on rung two</h2>
      <p>
        Rung two looks like the safe middle and is the most dangerous place on
        the ladder. It is where you hold approval rights without review
        capacity: the machine holds the reasoning, you hold the liability. Most
        enterprise AI deployment lives here, and it is
        <a class="link" href="/topics/responsibility">the crumple-zone recipe</a>.
        Rung two is only honest when the three-question test passes — you have the
        information to disagree, the time to disagree, and the standing to
        disagree without penalty.
      </p>

      <h2>Where each topic lands</h2>
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Topic</th><th>Rung</th><th>Why</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              PLACEMENTS.map((p) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <a class="link" href={`/topics/${p.slug}`}>
                      {byId[p.slug]?.kicker ?? p.slug}
                    </a>
                  </td>
                  <td style="white-space:nowrap; font-family:var(--mono); font-size:0.86rem">{p.rung}</td>
                  <td>{p.why}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>What the ladder is not</h2>
      <p>
        It is not a moral ranking. Rung five is not virtuous and rung one is not
        a failure — a great deal of what you do should be on rung one, and the
        productivity of rung three is real and large. The ladder exists to make
        the choice <em>explicit</em>, because the default is drift: everything
        slides down toward rung one, one convenient decision at a time, and
        nobody ever decides to let their judgment go.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .lhead { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem) 1rem; max-width: 46rem; }
  .lhead h1 { font-size: var(--t-h1); margin-block: 1rem 1.2rem; }
  .lhead__sub { color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 54ch; }
  .lbody { max-width: var(--measure); padding-bottom: 5rem; }
  .lbody h2 {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    margin-block: 3rem 0.9rem;
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  .lbody h2::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 2.4rem;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--human);
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
  }
  .lbody p { margin-bottom: 1.1em; }
  .rules { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 1.6rem 0 0; counter-reset: r; }
  .rules li {
    counter-increment: r;
    padding: 1.4rem 0 1.4rem 2.6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    position: relative;
  }
  .rules li:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .rules li::before {
    content: counter(r, decimal-leading-zero);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 1.75rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--human);
  }
  .rules__q { display: block; font-weight: 620; margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  .rules__a { display: block; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.6; }
  .tablewrap { overflow-x: auto; margin-block: 1.6rem; }
</style>
